{"name":"Bloodthirsty Vampire","hex":"#9b0503","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bloodthirsty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Thirsty for blood: inexorably violent or eager for bloodshed; murderous.","Of a book, film, etc.: depicting much violence; gory, violent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bloodthirsty"},{"term":"vampire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mythological creature (usually humanoid and undead) said to feed on the blood or life energy of the living. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A person with habits traditionally ascribed to (literal) vampires, such as heliophobia, being a night owl, having pale skin, and so on."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To drain of energy or resources."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vampire"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bloodthirsty","blocks":[{"text":"Bloodthirsty is a 1970s Japanese vampire film trilogy produced by Toho Studio, consisting of three of their 1970s horror films: The Vampire Doll, Lake of Dracula and Evil of Dracula. While there is no plot connection between the films, they share a vampire theme. The films were all directed by Michio Yamamoto and co-written by Ei Ogawa."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloodthirsty","wikidata":"Q11625942"},{"title":"Vampire","blocks":[{"text":"A vampire is a mythical creature with a human-like appearance that subsists by feeding on the vital essence of humans. In European folklore, vampires are undead humanoid creatures that visit loved ones and cause mischief or deaths in the neighbourhoods they inhabited while they were alive. They wear shrouds and are often described as bloated and of ruddy or dark countenance, contrasting with the modern gaunt, pale vampire which dates from the early 19th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg/330px-Philip_Burne-Jones_-_The_Vampire.jpg?utm_source=en.